+# #######################################
+# ####### Push Notifications (Bark) #####
+# #######################################
+
+# Self-hosted deployment push channel. The OSS notification slots are no-ops by
+# default (Cloud overrides them with its own multi-channel service); set
+# BARK_KEY to get inbox rows plus an iOS push without Expo/APNs credentials.
+#
+# Only the device is configured here. How a notification presents itself —
+# interruption level, collapse group, icon — is decided per scenario in code
+# (packages/business-server/src/notification/levels.ts), not by the deployment.
+#
+# Requires APP_URL to be your reachable public origin: the deep link tapped on
+# the phone is built from it, and its unset fallback (http://localhost:3210)
+# resolves against the phone itself.
+#
+# Bark device key — the path segment of https://api.day.app/<key>
+# BARK_KEY=
+# Self-hosted Bark server. Defaults to the public Bark service.
+# BARK_SERVER_URL=https://api.day.app
+# Restricts delivery to one account. Leave unset only on single-user instances:
+# without it every user's notifications land on the configured phone.
+# Find the id with `lh whoami`, or: select id from users where email = 'you@example.com';
+# BARK_USER_ID=
+#
+# To check the channel itself, bypass LobeHub entirely:
+#   curl -X POST https://api.day.app/<BARK_KEY> -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+#     -d '{"title":"LobeHub","body":"test"}'
+# A silent deployment usually means BARK_USER_ID does not match the account that
+# triggered the run — run with DEBUG=lobe-notification to see why a push was skipped.
